From b76e1ab279990995692fa74996271934f0fef4f4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <tristan75015@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 9 Oct 2026 17:48:54 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Integration of the behaviours by batches

New parameter of the non linear evolution problems,
`BehaviourIntegration`: `PointByPoint` (default, the current path) or
`Batch`. By batches, the gradients are computed element by element, then
each behaviour integrator integrates its behaviour at all its points with
a single call to MGIS (`mgis::behaviour::integrate` on the material data
manager): point by point in MGIS, up to the first failure, for a
behaviour generated by the `generic` interface; as a batch of all the
points (openmp, stlpar... backends) for a behaviour generated by the
`generic-parallel` interface, which can only be integrated in this mode.
The default value can be given by the environment variable
`MFEM_MGIS_BEHAVIOUR_INTEGRATION`, to run a test suite or an application
in this mode.

- `AbstractBehaviourIntegrator`: `supportsBatchIntegration`,
  `computeGradients` and `integrateBatch`, with default implementations
  (no support).
- Standard and FBar CRTP bases: the computation of the gradients at a
  point is extracted from `implementIntegrate`, which now shares it with
  `computeGradients`; `integrateBatch` rotates the tangent operator
  blocks after the call to MGIS.
- `BehaviourIntegratorBase::integrateAllIntegrationPoints`: the material
  properties at the beginning of the time step given to the behaviour
  are the ones at the end of the time step, as in the integration point
  by point (they are restored after the call).
- Refused with a message: `Batch` with PETSc, a behaviour integrator
  without batch support (micromorphic damage), a behaviour generated by
  the generic-parallel interface point by point, an orthotropic
  behaviour generated by the generic-parallel interface (its library
  only provides the rotations of batches).

BatchIntegrationTest: the same results, to the last bit, point by point
and by batches for a plastic behaviour, a finite strain behaviour, an
orthotropic behaviour, material properties given at the end of the time
step only with a spatial variation, the FBar formulation, and the plastic
behaviour generated by the generic-parallel interface with the openmp and
stlpar backends; refusals; failure of the integration at some points
(new test behaviour FailingIsotropicLinearElasticity). The whole test
suite passes by batches except the refused cases.
